      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the product of the roots of the polynomial equation \\(3x^3 - x^2 - 20x + 27 = 0\\), we can use Vieta's formulas. Vieta's formulas relate the coefficients of a polynomial to sums and products of its roots.\n\nFor a general polynomial of the form \\(ax^n + bx^{n-1} + \\cdots + k = 0\\), the product of the roots is given by \\((-1)^n \\frac{k}{a}\\), where \\(k\\) is the constant term and \\(a\\) is the leading coefficient.\n\nIn the given polynomial \\(3x^3 - x^2 - 20x + 27 = 0\\):\n- The leading coefficient \\(a\\) is 3.\n- The constant term \\(k\\) is 27.\n\nSince the polynomial is of degree 3 (\\(n = 3\\)), we substitute \\(n = 3\\) into the formula for the product of the roots:\n\\[\n\\text{Product of the roots} = (-1)^3 \\frac{27}{3}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression:\n\\[\n(-1)^3 = -1\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{27}{3} = 9\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Product of the roots} = -1 \\times 9 = -9\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of the roots of the equation \\(3x^3 - x^2 - 20x + 27 = 0\\) is \\(\\boxed{-9}\\).",
