      "text": "To find the product of the roots of the polynomial equation \\(3x^3 - x^2 - 20x + 27 = 0\\), we can use Vieta's formulas. Vieta's formulas relate the coefficients of a polynomial to sums and products of its roots.\n\nFor a general polynomial of the form \\(ax^n + bx^{n-1} + \\cdots + k = 0\\), Vieta's formulas tell us that the product of the roots is given by \\((-1)^n \\frac{k}{a}\\), where \\(k\\) is the constant term and \\(a\\) is the leading coefficient.\n\nIn the given polynomial \\(3x^3 - x^2 - 20x + 27 = 0\\), the coefficients are:\n- \\(a = 3\\) (leading coefficient),\n- \\(b = -1\\) (coefficient of \\(x^2\\)),\n- \\(c = -20\\) (coefficient of \\(x\\)),\n- \\(d = 27\\) (constant term).\n\nThe product of the roots of the polynomial is given by:\n\\[\n(-1)^3 \\frac{27}{3} = -1 \\cdot \\frac{27}{3} = -9.\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of the roots of the equation \\(3x^3 - x^2 - 20x + 27 = 0\\) is \\(\\boxed{-9}\\).",
